this is a production of cornell  university  all right we are up in northern new york  in the thousand islands region  uh looking at some vines today and  behind me we have marquette  and i've probably talked about marquette  in class  it's a minnesota cultivar uh  it's red makes quite nice wines we've  actually got the start of  raisin here so we can see the start of  grape ripening  today is uh july 30th i think it is  so marquette's a very early cultivar  because it has some vitus riparian in it  so we are actually seeing some berries  starting to turn purple  as you can see and they're softening too  so if i  squeeze the darker berries they're very  soft  the green berries are still very hard  so start of raisin in here but what i  really wanted to show you  was that we have a lot of winter damage  in this vineyard  and uh when we've had a really cold  event  through the winter it's a really good  time to go back and look at site  selection  and look at well what could i have  learned or what could i do better  so behind me here we have a slight  uphill slope  and the vines look look pretty decent  we've got pretty good  growth uh coming along here some nice  shoots  down to about my waist or so this is on  high wire cordon  but if we turn around and walk this way  and head down the hill you can actually  see  a fair amount of damage so  here for example uh you can see very  small berries that uh they are purple  but they're not  ripening so what's happened here is that  this vine has started to grow  but there was trunk damage that wasn't  terribly visible  at the time so the vine starts to grow  looks okay  and then we see some damage like this  and you can actually see it looks like  the trunk is going to split  right along here and so we're going to  lose at least the top  part of this vine uh the good news is  the vine will not need to be replanted  we have some pretty good uh suckers down  here or renewals  so what uh we can do is just cut off the  trunk  and then tie one of these up and they  will become the new  vine so no need to replant here  but as we walk downhill you can see  that things actually get a little bit  worse we've got some decent vines  and then here we come to a vine that has  nothing alive on the top  but again just some suckers here for  trunk renewal  what this grower really needs to do is  thin these out so right now we've got  one two  three four five six seven eight nine  suckers  on this vine and if we've got nine  suckers  they're all gonna grow to say about this  height if we thin that down to maybe two  suckers then the carbohydrates in the  roots  are going to go into those two suckers  only and we'll get really good growth  and get this at least up to the top wire  as a vine replacement for the following  year  so again no need to replant but it's  going to be a couple of years until  these vines look  reasonably normal and then again if we  just had a little further down the hill  we can see more and more damage here on  both the left side  and the right side and what i want to  show you is at the very bottom here is  we hit a bowl in the vineyard  and we can actually see we've had some  drainage problems here  too so if you look down here uh  no vines they've lost them in a previous  year  and then there are some tire tracks  right down here on the ground  some considerable divots which shows us  that it's been very very  wet here and there's no tile drainage  under this  vineyard so there's nowhere for the  water to go so when it's wet  it just cools here so water pools here  cold air pools here and so that's why  there's no  vines you can see on this neighboring  vine over here  we've got some very small suckers uh and  even if we thin these out they're not  going to do terribly well  so this is when you can go back and  really look at your vineyard design  and look at what could have been done  better in this situation i think this  low area  definitely needs some tile drainage an